video: tegra: dc: ext: take buffer id 0 as "no buffer", as R32

JetPack 4's X driver shows its screens through tegradc's ext interface,
whose buffer ids are dma-buf file descriptors. R32's interface uses 0 for
"no buffer"; R35's uses a negative id, so it looked up file descriptor 0
for every unused plane and failed the flip.

Take 0 as none too, as R32 did; a negative id still means none. A buffer
that cannot be pinned now says why (rate limited) where every failure used
to return only -ENOMEM.

Tested on the Nano: in trial 22 X's flips reached the pin with no "not a
dma-buf", attach or map failure; from trial 23 its buffers show on screen.

Licence: GPL-2.0-only. Lab notes, with every measurement: NOTES.md.
